### What is the owning side in a bidirectional relationship?

A foreign key is a single column on ONE physical table. In an account-to-trades relationship, the trades table has an account_id column. There is no column on the accounts table pointing at trades. So in JPA terms, the child (Trade) is the natural OWNER of the relationship because its table physically stores the foreign key.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

@Entity
@Table(name = "trades")
public class Trade {

    @Id
    @Column(name = "trade_id")
    private String tradeId;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)    // override default EAGER
    @JoinColumn(name = "account_id")      // the actual FK column on trades table
    private Account account;

    @Column(name = "amount", precision = 15, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal amount;

    @Column(name = "status")
    private String status;

    // getters, setters
}
```

```text
Trade is the OWNING side because:
- trades table has the account_id column (the FK)
- @JoinColumn tells Hibernate the exact FK column name
- Hibernate reads this field to decide what value to write into account_id
```

### What is the inverse side with mappedBy?

The parent entity (Account) references a collection of trades with @OneToMany(mappedBy="account"). The value of mappedBy is the NAME OF THE FIELD on the child entity that owns the relationship. It is NOT a column name. mappedBy tells Hibernate: I am not the owner; the foreign key lives over there.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
@Table(name = "accounts")
public class Account {

    @Id
    @Column(name = "account_id")
    private String accountId;

    @Column(name = "holder_name", nullable = false)
    private String holderName;

    @Column(name = "balance", precision = 15, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal balance;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "account", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List<Trade> trades = new ArrayList<>();

    // getters, setters, helpers
}
```

```text
Account is the INVERSE side because:
- accounts table has NO column pointing at trades
- mappedBy = "account" points to the Trade.account field (the owning side)
- Hibernate does NOT generate SQL from changes to this collection alone
```

### Why must you keep both sides in sync?

Because only the owning side drives the foreign-key SQL, if you add a trade only to the parent's collection and forget to set the child's account field, Hibernate writes a row with a NULL account_id. The fix is synchronization helpers that update both sides at once.

```java
// WRONG: only updating the inverse side
package com.bank.service;

import com.bank.model.Account;
import com.bank.model.Trade;

public class WrongExample {
    public void addTrade(Account account, Trade trade) {
        account.getTrades().add(trade);   // inverse side only
        // trade.account is still null!
        // Hibernate writes: INSERT INTO trades (trade_id, amount, account_id) VALUES ('T1', 1000, NULL)
    }
}
```

```java
// CORRECT: synchronization helper updates both sides
package com.bank.model;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public class Account {
    // ... fields ...

    private List<Trade> trades = new ArrayList<>();

    public void addTrade(Trade trade) {
        trades.add(trade);           // inverse side (in-memory navigation)
        trade.setAccount(this);      // OWNING side (what Hibernate persists)
    }

    public void removeTrade(Trade trade) {
        trades.remove(trade);
        trade.setAccount(null);      // orphanRemoval -> DELETE on flush
    }
}
```

```text
CORRECT result:
INSERT INTO trades (trade_id, amount, account_id) VALUES ('T1', 1000, 'SAV2001')
account_id is set because trade.setAccount(this) was called.
```

### What does @JoinColumn control?

@JoinColumn specifies the name of the foreign-key column on the owning side's table. It also controls whether the column is nullable, unique, insertable, or updatable. Without @JoinColumn, Hibernate uses a default name derived from the field name and the target entity's id column.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;

@Entity
public class Trade {

    @Id
    private String tradeId;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(
        name = "account_id",          // FK column name
        nullable = false,              // NOT NULL constraint
        foreignKey = @ForeignKey(name = "fk_trade_account")  // named FK constraint
    )
    private Account account;
}
```

```text
Generated DDL:
ALTER TABLE trades
  ADD CONSTRAINT fk_trade_account
  FOREIGN KEY (account_id) REFERENCES accounts(account_id);
```

### How does fetch type affect the relationship?

@ManyToOne defaults to EAGER, meaning loading a trade always loads its account in a second query. This is almost always wrong in production. Override it to LAZY. @OneToMany already defaults to LAZY. When you genuinely need the parent with its children, use a JOIN FETCH query.

```java
// WRONG: @ManyToOne default EAGER causes unnecessary fetches
@ManyToOne  // default FetchType.EAGER
@JoinColumn(name = "account_id")
private Account account;  // loading ANY trade also loads the full Account

// CORRECT: override to LAZY
@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
@JoinColumn(name = "account_id")
private Account account;  // Account loaded only when accessed
```

```java
package com.bank.repository;

import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.JpaRepository;
import org.springframework.data.jpa.repository.Query;
import com.bank.model.Account;
import java.util.Optional;

public interface AccountRepository extends JpaRepository<Account, String> {

    @Query("SELECT a FROM Account a LEFT JOIN FETCH a.trades WHERE a.accountId = :id")
    Optional<Account> findWithTrades(String id);
    // One query fetches Account + all its Trades -- no N+1
}
```

### What is a unidirectional alternative?

If the parent does not need to navigate to its children, you can put @OneToMany @JoinColumn on the parent without any field on the child. But this is less efficient because Hibernate issues extra UPDATE statements to set the foreign key after inserting children.

```java
// Unidirectional @OneToMany (less efficient)
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

@Entity
public class Account {

    @Id
    private String accountId;

    @OneToMany
    @JoinColumn(name = "account_id")  // no mappedBy, no field on Trade
    private List<Trade> trades = new ArrayList<>();
    // Hibernate: INSERT trade (NULL FK), then UPDATE trade SET account_id = 'SAV2001'
    // Extra UPDATE per child row -> bidirectional with mappedBy is preferred
}
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is adding a child only to the parent's collection and forgetting to set the owning reference. A second mistake is confusing mappedBy with a column name.

```java
// WRONG: mappedBy points to a column name instead of a field name
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "account_id")   // WRONG! "account_id" is a column, not a field
private List<Trade> trades;

// CORRECT: mappedBy points to the Java field name on Trade
@OneToMany(mappedBy = "account")       // CORRECT! "account" is the field on Trade
private List<Trade> trades;
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

A senior engineer always overrides @ManyToOne from EAGER to LAZY, uses JOIN FETCH when the association is needed, applies cascade only to genuinely owned children, and implements equals/hashCode on a stable business key rather than the generated primary key for Set-based collections.

```java
package com.bank.model;

import jakarta.persistence.*;
import java.util.Objects;

@Entity
public class Trade {

    @Id
    private String tradeId;

    @ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)
    @JoinColumn(name = "account_id")
    private Account account;

    // equals/hashCode on stable business key (tradeId), not generated PK
    @Override
    public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof Trade other)) return false;
        return tradeId != null && tradeId.equals(other.tradeId);
    }

    @Override
    public int hashCode() {
        return Objects.hashCode(tradeId);
    }
}
```
